-- =====================================================================
-- Strive Elite - College Recruiting and Academy Recruiting
-- =====================================================================
-- Coach Yinka, Oct 29 2026: "Recruiting isn't seperate from college and
-- academy. We need a college recruiting course and an academy recruiting
-- course."
--
-- The Recruiting kit (045: the CV, the five emails, the tape, the rules,
-- the tracker) is folded into the two component courses of 047, so each
-- route carries its own templates in its own words (grad year on the
-- college route, birth year on the academy route; a director's email and
-- the tryout follow-up on the academy route). Two NEW courses, by slug:
-- college-recruiting (11 components, 24 pages) and academy-recruiting
-- (12 components, 20 pages), both lesson courses, seeded ON because they
-- replace three courses that are live today. The old recruiting, college
-- and academy courses are switched OFF and sorted to the bottom, never
-- deleted here: a coach deletes them in the editor at /coach/drills once
-- the new two are read. Idempotent: an existing slug is left alone, so a
-- coach's later edits are never overwritten; each block refuses a partial
-- result. No contact-rule dates, no alumni, never a roster, scholarship
-- or academy place promised.
-- =====================================================================

do $$
declare
  cid uuid;
  placed int;
begin
  if exists (select 1 from public.elite_courses where slug = 'college-recruiting') then
    return;
  end if;
  insert into public.elite_courses (slug, name, subtitle, intro, kind, active, sort)
  values ('college-recruiting', 'College Recruiting', 'The college route, part by part.', E'Grades open the door. Film gets you watched. The club coach''s phone call closes it.\nEvery part below is its own job. Work them side by side, not one after another.\nThe player writes every email. Copy the template, fill the brackets, send it yourself.', 'lesson', true, 50)
  returning id into cid;
  insert into public.elite_course_steps (course_id, level_no, level_name, title, body, sort) values
    (cid, 1, 'Start here', 'The levels', E'Division I, Division II, Division III, NAIA, and junior college. Every one of them is real college soccer with real coaches recruiting real players.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nDivision III gives no athletic money. It often gives academic money, and the soccer can be very good. Do not cross it off because of the name.\n\nJunior college is two years, then a transfer. For a late developer or a player whose grades need a reset, it is a route, not a step down.\n\nThe division is not the goal. The goal is a program where you get on the field in your first two years and a school you would stay at if soccer ended tomorrow.\n\nWatch two games of any program before you email them. If you cannot picture yourself in that team''s shape, move on.', 10),
    (cid, 1, 'Start here', 'The five checks', E'What a college coach checks, in this order. One weak link and the process stops there. Each check has its own part of this course.\n\n1. GRADES. A player they cannot get admitted is not a player. Transcript first, every time. See Grades.\n\n2. POSITION NEED. Are they losing a player in your position in your graduation year? If not, you are a backup plan at best. See The school list.\n\n3. THE TAPE, THEN THE FULL GAME. The tape earns the click. The full game earns the phone call. See Film.\n\n4. THE PHONE CALL TO YOUR CLUB COACH. What kind of teammate you are. Whether you train. Whether your parents are a problem. See Outreach.\n\n5. SPEED AND SIZE FOR THE POSITION. Honest answer: this matters more the higher the division. That is the training, and it is what your week in this app is for.\n\nThe CV carries all five on one page. See The CV.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nGet all five ready before the first email goes out. A coach who finds a weak link does not come back to check again later.', 20),
    (cid, 2, 'Grades', 'Grades open the door', E'A coach cannot recruit a player the school will not admit. Grades are checked before film, before anything.\n\nWHAT TO HAVE READY\n\nYour transcript, current. Your GPA. Your graduation year. Test scores if you have them and the school asks for them.\n\nA one-line academic summary on the CV: school, GPA, grad year, anything a coach can use (honors, AP classes).\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nIf you are aiming at Division I or II, register with the NCAA Eligibility Center during high school. Your school counselor knows which courses count. Do it early; it is paperwork, and paperwork late is a problem.\n\nGrades are money. Academic aid is often bigger than soccer money, and it is open to every player on the roster.\n\nA bad semester is not the end. A bad transcript with no plan is. If grades slipped, fix them first and let the coach see the trend going the right way.\n\nTell us if grades are the weak link. That changes which schools go on the list, and we would rather know now.', 10),
    (cid, 3, 'The CV', 'The one-page CV', E'One page. If it runs onto a second page, cut it. A coach gives it thirty seconds.\n\nTEMPLATE\n\n[Full name]\n[Graduation year] | [Position, then second position] | [Preferred foot]\n[Height] | [Date of birth]\n[City, State] | [Email the player checks] | [Phone]\nTape: [link] | Full games: [link]\n\nCLUB\n[Club name], [team and league, e.g. U15 MLS NEXT], [years], [role: starter, captain]\n[Previous club], [team and league], [years]\n\nSCHOOL\n[High school], [GPA], [graduation year], [SAT or ACT if taken]\n[Honors, AP classes, anything academic a coach can use]\n\nHONORS\n[Only real, dated ones: All-Conference 2026, ODP pool 2025, team captain 2026]\n\nCOACH REFERENCES\n[Club coach name], [club], [phone], [email]\n[Second coach or trainer], [phone], [email]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nFacts only. No adjectives about yourself. "Hard worker" means nothing on paper; a GPA and a league do.\n\nPut the tape link at the top. It is the only line most coaches click.\n\nUpdate it every season. A CV from last year reads like you stopped caring.\n\nSave it as a PDF named [Last name]-[First name]-[Grad year].pdf. Never a Word file, never a photo of a page.\n\nAsk your coach references before you list them. Every time.', 10),
    (cid, 4, 'Film', 'The tape and the full game', E'College coaches watch a tape on a phone between sessions and decide in the first minute whether to keep going. Three to four minutes, your best five clips in the first forty seconds. The full game is what they watch when they are serious.\n\nWHAT GOES IN\n\nYour position''s job, over and over. A striker''s tape is goals and the runs before them. A center back''s tape is duels, headers, first passes out. A midfielder''s tape is receiving under pressure and what happens next.\n\nEvery clip starts a few seconds before your touch so the coach sees the picture you saw.\n\nMark yourself at the start of every clip: a circle or an arrow for the first second, then gone.\n\nWHAT STAYS OUT\n\nMusic. Most coaches watch on mute, and the ones who do not want to hear the game.\n\nSlow motion, effects, titles between clips. One title card at the start with your name, grad year, position, club, email. Nothing else.\n\nClips from training. Game footage only. A trick against a cone proves nothing.\n\nAnything longer than four minutes. A coach who wants more will ask for the full game.\n\nTHE FULL GAME\n\nAlways have one full match available as a link. Unedited. Put the link under the tape link in every email. The tape gets the door open; the full game gets you through it.\n\nHOSTING\n\nUnlisted on a video site, link in the CV and every email. Check the link works from a phone that is not yours before you send it.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNew tape every season, newest clips first. Title it with the season: [Name] [Position] [Grad year] | Fall 2026. A coach can tell in one line whether it is current.\n\nFilm against the best team on your schedule. Coaches know who you played.\n\nAt this level the full game matters more than anywhere else: it shows what you do off the ball, which a tape hides.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'The school list', 'Twenty across three levels', E'Five stretch. Ten fit. Five that would say yes today. Email all twenty.\n\nFOR EACH SCHOOL, ANSWER\n\nDo they recruit my position in my graduation year?\nCould I get in on grades alone?\nCould my family afford it without soccer money?\nWould I be happy here if I never played a minute?\nHave I watched them play?\n\nIf two answers are no, take it off the list.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nFit is not a consolation prize. The fit schools are where most players actually play.\n\nThe list changes. A reply moves a school up. Silence after two follow-ups moves it out. Twenty rows, always.\n\nTrack every school on the tracker, under The rules. One row each.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'The school list', 'Position need', E'The roster page is public. Read it before a single email goes out.\n\nHOW TO READ A ROSTER\n\nCount the players in your position. Note each one''s year. A team with four of you in the year above is not recruiting you; a team losing two of you the year you arrive is.\n\nLook at who played. Minutes are usually on the stats page. A roster with twelve midfielders and three who play tells you where the real competition is.\n\nCheck last year''s recruiting class. The positions they took last year are the positions they are less likely to take again.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nPosition need changes every spring. Re-read the roster of every school on the list once a year.\n\nIf a school on the list has no need in your year, it is a stretch school whatever its level. Keep it or swap it, but know which it is.', 20),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'Which email, when', E'The player writes every email. Never the parent. The templates are the next five pages; this one is which, and when.\n\nWHICH TEMPLATE, WHEN\n\nThe first email to a coach: once you have the tape, the full game, the CV and your grades ready. One coach, one email, one true line about their program.\n\nBefore a game or showcase: a week out, then two lines the morning of.\n\nAfter a game they watched: within a day. Especially if you played badly.\n\nThe follow-up when nobody replies: two weeks later, with something new. Two of these, then only with news.\n\nAfter an ID camp or a visit: the same evening.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nSend on a weekday morning from an address that is your name.\n\nUnder 150 words. The links do the talking.\n\nNo reply is not a no. At some levels a coach is not allowed to write back to you yet. They still read. Ask us before you read a silence as a no.', 10),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'The first email to a coach', E'Send this before anything else. One coach, one email, written by the player. Never a mass email, never from a parent''s address.\n\nSUBJECT: [Grad year] [Position] | [Club, league] | [Your name]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nMy name is [First Last], a [grad year] [position] with [club] in [league]. I am writing because I want to play at [school] and I have been following [one true specific thing: how the team plays, a recent result, a player who came from a club like mine].\n\nHere is my tape: [link]\nFull game: [link]\nCV attached.\n\nI will be at [event] on [date], field [number], kickoff [time], wearing [number], if you are able to watch. If not, I would be grateful for any feedback on the tape.\n\nThank you for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Grad year], [Position]\n[Phone]\n[Club coach name], [phone] (reference)\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nOne specific true line about their program. That is the whole difference between an email that gets opened and one that gets deleted.\n\nUnder 150 words. Everything a coach needs is in the links and the attachment.\n\nSend it from an email address that is your name, not a nickname.\n\nSend it on a weekday morning. Not Friday afternoon, not game day.', 20),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'Before a game or showcase', E'Send this five to seven days before the event, and again the morning of, two lines only.\n\nSUBJECT: [Event], [date] | [Your name], [grad year] [position]\n\nTEMPLATE, A WEEK OUT\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nI wanted to let you know I will be playing at [event] on [date].\n\n[Team] vs [opponent]\n[Day, date], kickoff [time]\n[Venue], field [number]\nJersey number [number], [position]\n\nMy tape is here if useful before the game: [link]\n\nI would love for you to watch. Thank you.\n\n[First Last]\n[Phone]\n\nTEMPLATE, THE MORNING OF\n\nCoach [Last name], playing today at [time] on field [number], number [number]. Hope to see you there. [First Last]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nGive them everything they need to find you in ten seconds: field, time, number. Coaches walk between six fields.\n\nNever ask if they are coming. Tell them where you will be.\n\nIf the schedule changes, send one more line. A coach who walks to the wrong field does not come back.', 30),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'After a game they watched', E'Send this within 24 hours. Even if you played badly. Especially if you played badly.\n\nSUBJECT: Thank you for watching, [event]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nThank you for coming to watch [team] vs [opponent] on [date].\n\n[One honest line about the game: what went well, or what you know you need to fix. Never an excuse.]\n\nI am very interested in [school or club]. If you have any feedback, or if there is a next step you would like me to take, I would be grateful to hear it.\n\n[Updated tape link, if you have new clips from that game]\n\nThank you again for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Phone]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNever blame the referee, the pitch, a teammate or the coach. A coach who reads an excuse stops reading.\n\nOne honest line about your own game shows more maturity than any highlight.\n\nIf they gave you feedback at the field, repeat it back: "You mentioned my first touch under pressure. I am working on it." Coaches remember who listened.', 40),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'The follow-up when nobody replies', E'Most coaches do not reply to the first email. That is normal. Send this two weeks after the last one.\n\nSUBJECT: Following up | [Your name], [grad year] [position]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nI wanted to follow up on my email from [date]. I remain very interested in [school or club].\n\nSince then: [one new thing, true: a new tape, a result, a grade, an event you will be at].\n\nTape: [link]\n\nThank you for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Phone]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAlways bring something new. "Just checking in" with nothing new gets deleted.\n\nTwo follow-ups, two weeks apart. After that, leave it until you have a real update or a new event near them.\n\nNo reply is not a no. Coaches are not allowed to reply to younger players at some levels, and they still read. Keep the emails coming at each real update.', 50),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'After an ID camp or a visit', E'Send this the same evening or the next morning.\n\nSUBJECT: Thank you, [camp or visit], [date]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nThank you for [the camp, the visit, the time you gave me] on [date].\n\n[One specific thing you took from it: a drill, something they said, something about the program you saw.]\n\n[If true: After seeing it in person, [school] is where I want to play.]\n\nMy tape: [link]\n\nI will keep you updated as the season goes. Thank you again.\n\n[First Last]\n[Grad year], [Position]\n[Phone]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nName the specific thing. "Great camp" says nothing. "The possession game in the second session showed me how you want center backs to play" says you were paying attention.\n\nSay you want it only if you mean it. Coaches compare notes.', 60),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'The club coach''s call', E'A college coach who likes the tape calls your club coach. What they hear in that call decides more than the tape did.\n\nWHAT THEY ASK\n\nDo they train. Are they coachable. How do they handle being benched. What are the parents like. Would you take them again.\n\nMAKE THE CALL EASY\n\nTell your club coach which schools you are emailing. Ask them to expect the call.\n\nPut their phone number on the CV, with their permission, every time.\n\nGive them one line about each school: level, why you want it, your grade situation. A coach who sounds informed sounds like a coach who believes in you.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nYour club coach is a reference every day, not just on the phone. The way you train in October is what they will say in March.\n\nIf your club coach would not make the call for you, that is the first problem to fix, before any email.', 70),
    (cid, 7, 'Exposure', 'ID camps', E'A camp is an evaluation, not a lesson.\n\nGO ONLY WHERE\n\nA coach has replied to you, or the staff of a school on your list is actually working the camp. Check the staff list. "Guest coaches" is often one assistant.\n\nON THE DAY\n\nFirst ten minutes: simple, sharp, loud. Play your position. Nobody gets recruited for a trick.\n\nThank the coach who ran your group, by name.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nEmail a week before and the evening after. The templates are under Outreach.\n\nOne camp where a coach wants to see you beats five where nobody knows your name.', 10),
    (cid, 7, 'Exposure', 'Showcases', E'Coaches walk between six fields with a list. If you are not on the list before the event, you are background.\n\nBEFORE\n\nSend the schedule a week out to every coach on your list who is attending: field, time, number. Morning-of reminder, two lines.\n\nConfirm which coaches are actually attending. The event''s coach list is public and changes the week of.\n\nDURING\n\nPlay your position the way your club coach coaches it. Communicate. Lose a ball, win it back.\n\nAFTER\n\nWithin a day, the After a game they watched email to every coach who came. One honest line about your own game, never an excuse.', 20),
    (cid, 7, 'Exposure', 'The money test', E'Before paying for any camp, showcase or tournament, ask two questions.\n\nWhich coaches on my list will be there?\nHave I emailed them?\n\nIf the answer to either is none, keep the money.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nExposure is not being seen by someone. It is being seen by a coach who was already looking for you.\n\nThe same money spent on one more season of film, or one unofficial visit, often does more.', 30),
    (cid, 8, 'The visit', 'Unofficial visits', E'Any family can walk a campus, watch a training session and sit in a game. No invitation needed.\n\nHOW TO DO IT\n\nEmail the coach a week out: you are visiting on [date], could you watch training. Even a no tells you something.\n\nWatch a training session, not just a game. Training tells you how they coach.\n\nTalk to a current player without the coach in the room.\n\nSit in a class if the school allows it. You are choosing a school, not only a team.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nVisit a fit school before a stretch school. You will know what you are comparing against.\n\nEmail the same evening: the After an ID camp or a visit template under Outreach.', 10),
    (cid, 8, 'The visit', 'What to ask a coach', E'Ask these out loud, the player asking, not the parent.\n\nWhere do you see me in your roster in year one? Year two?\nHow many players in my position are in my graduation year?\nWhat does a normal week look like in season and out of season?\nWhat happens academically if I am injured?\nWhat support is there for grades and scheduling?\nWhat is the money, year by year, and is it in writing?\nWho leaves this program, and why?\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAn answer you do not like now is a gift. An answer you do not like after you sign is a problem.\n\nWrite the answers down the same day, on the tracker.', 20),
    (cid, 9, 'Money and the decision', 'Money, plainly', E'Men''s college soccer money is usually partial. Academic money is often bigger than athletic money. Treat them as one number.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAsk for the offer in writing, year by year. What it is, what it depends on, what happens if you are injured or cut.\n\nAsk what the full cost of attendance is, not the tuition line. Housing, food, travel, fees.\n\nNever choose a school on soccer money alone. Choose the school, then make the money work, then play.\n\nNobody can promise a roster spot or a scholarship. What the work earns is a clear path and a player ready for the moment.', 10),
    (cid, 9, 'Money and the decision', 'Deciding', E'When more than one door is open, use the same five questions from the school list, in this order.\n\nWill I play in my first two years?\nWould I stay if soccer ended tomorrow?\nCan my family afford it with the offer in writing?\nDid I like the coach and the players when the coach was not in the room?\nHave I seen them play and train?\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAdmissions deadlines are real and earlier than you think. The soccer offer means nothing if the application is late.\n\nTell every other coach on your list when you decide. Short, grateful, the same day. Coaches move jobs and remember who was straight with them.\n\nKeep playing like someone is watching. Transfers and late offers happen every year.', 20),
    (cid, 10, 'The rules', 'The rules nobody tells you', E'THE PLAYER DOES THE WORK\n\nThe player writes every email. The player answers the phone. A coach who hears from a parent first assumes the player does not want it enough.\n\nParents: proofread, drive, pay for the tape. That is the job.\n\nTIMING\n\nEmail a coach when you have something: a new tape, an event near them, a result, a grade. Not on a schedule, not when you are bored.\n\nA week before an event, then the morning of. Within a day after. Two weeks later if silence. Then only with news.\n\nBEFORE THE FIRST EMAIL\n\nTape, full game, grades, CV, and a club coach who knows where you are aiming. One weak link and the process stops there, and a coach who finds it does not come back to check again later.\n\nSOCIAL MEDIA\n\nAssume every coach reads your accounts before they reply. Clean it up before the first email, not after.\n\nWHAT WE NEVER PROMISE\n\nNobody can promise a roster spot or a scholarship, not us, not anyone. What the work promises is a clear path and a player ready for the moment. That is the whole job.', 10),
    (cid, 10, 'The rules', 'The tracker', E'One row per school. Update it the day anything happens. If it is not on the sheet, it did not happen.\n\nCOLUMNS\n\nSchool | Level (stretch, fit, yes) | Coach name | Coach email | First email sent | Replied (date) | Last contact | Next step | Next step date | Notes\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNext step is never blank. Even "follow up Nov 12" or "send fall tape when done" counts.\n\nRead the sheet every Sunday. Five minutes. Do whatever is due that week.\n\nTwenty rows. When one goes cold, add one. When one says yes, keep the others warm until you have decided.\n\nThe sheet belongs to the player. The parent can see it. The player keeps it.', 20),
    (cid, 11, 'The timeline', 'By grade', E'Every part of this course runs at once. This is what each year leans on. No dates, because the rules on when coaches may write to you change by division and year. Ask us for the current rules.\n\nGRADE 9\nGrades. Build the film habit: film every game, keep the best clips in one folder. Start the list of twenty. Go watch a college game.\n\nGRADE 10\nFirst emails go out, from the player. ID camps only at schools that replied. Unofficial visits start. Tape updated after the season.\n\nGRADE 11\nThe heavy year. Showcases with the schedule sent a week out. Tape every season. Follow-ups on a rhythm. Narrow the twenty to eight by spring. Register with the Eligibility Center if you have not.\n\nGRADE 12\nDecide. Admissions deadlines first. Keep playing like someone is watching.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nLate is fine. Plenty of players start this in grade 11 and land well. Late and disorganised is the problem.', 10);
  select count(*) into placed from public.elite_course_steps where course_id = cid;
  if placed <> 24 then
    raise exception 'College Recruiting seed placed % of 24 pages', placed;
  end if;
end $$;

do $$
declare
  cid uuid;
  placed int;
begin
  if exists (select 1 from public.elite_courses where slug = 'academy-recruiting') then
    return;
  end if;
  insert into public.elite_courses (slug, name, subtitle, intro, kind, active, sort)
  values ('academy-recruiting', 'Academy Recruiting', 'MLS NEXT, ECNL and the pro clubs.', E'Academies find players in games, not inboxes.\nYour current league is the filter. Climb one rung at a time.\nThe player writes every email. Copy the template, fill the brackets, send it yourself.', 'lesson', true, 60)
  returning id into cid;
  insert into public.elite_course_steps (course_id, level_no, level_name, title, body, sort) values
    (cid, 1, 'Start here', 'The ladder', E'Rec. Travel. ECNL RL or MLS NEXT 2. ECNL or MLS NEXT. Then the pro academies, which take most of their players from that top rung.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nClimb one rung at a time. A player who dominates a rung gets seen. A player who scrapes onto a rung and sits on the bench disappears.\n\nPlaying time one rung down beats the bench one rung up. Film and development come from minutes.\n\nKnow which rung you are on today and which clubs sit on the rung above you. Those clubs are your list.', 10),
    (cid, 1, 'Start here', 'How academies find you', E'Academy and pathway clubs scout games and events. Your email is the reason someone comes to a game; the game is what gets you in.\n\nWHAT A DIRECTOR CHECKS\n\nYour league and your minutes. Your birth year. What your club coach says. Your report card. Then the tape, then the tryout.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nBe the best player in your league games, not the best player at the one tryout. Tryouts confirm what scouts already saw.\n\nBirth year, not grade. Academies run on birth years, and you compete against your year.\n\nEach check has its own part of this course.', 20),
    (cid, 2, 'Your league', 'Dominate the rung', E'The league you are in is the only film that matters to the rung above. Scouts watch league games; they rarely watch anything else.\n\nWHAT DOMINATING LOOKS LIKE\n\nStarting every game. Doing your position''s job every game, not just the good ones. Being the player your own coach trusts when the game is tight.\n\nWHEN TO MOVE CLUBS\n\nMove up when you are starting and dominant, not when you are good enough to make the next roster. Ask one question before any move: will I play?\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThe scout who comes to see a teammate sees you too. Play every game like that.\n\nA season on the bench at a bigger club costs a year of film and a year of development. Nobody gets scouted from the bench.', 10),
    (cid, 3, 'The club coach and the director', 'Directors call directors', E'At this level the club coach''s phone call matters more than anywhere. Directors call directors. Make sure yours knows where you want to go.\n\nWHAT TO DO\n\nTell your club coach and your club''s director which clubs you are aiming at, and why. Ask them to make the introduction when you are ready.\n\nAsk them the honest question: am I ready for that rung, and if not, what would make me ready? Write the answer down and train it.\n\nPut your coach''s number on the CV, with their permission.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nA director who hears about you from another director already believes you are real. A director who hears from your parent does not.\n\nIf your own coach would not make the call, fix that first. It is usually training, attitude or minutes, and all three are in your hands.', 10),
    (cid, 4, 'The CV', 'The one-page CV', E'One page. If it runs onto a second page, cut it. A director gives it thirty seconds. Birth year on the first line; academies run on birth years.\n\nTEMPLATE\n\n[Full name]\n[Birth year] | [Position, then second position] | [Preferred foot]\n[Height] | [Date of birth]\n[City, State] | [Email the player checks] | [Phone]\nTape: [link] | Full games: [link]\n\nCLUB\n[Club name], [team and league, e.g. U15 MLS NEXT], [years], [role: starter, captain]\n[Previous club], [team and league], [years]\n\nSCHOOL\n[High school], [GPA], [graduation year], [SAT or ACT if taken]\n[Honors, AP classes, anything academic a coach can use]\n\nHONORS\n[Only real, dated ones: All-Conference 2026, ODP pool 2025, team captain 2026]\n\nCOACH REFERENCES\n[Club coach name], [club], [phone], [email]\n[Second coach or trainer], [phone], [email]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nFacts only. No adjectives about yourself. "Hard worker" means nothing on paper; a GPA and a league do.\n\nPut the tape link at the top. It is the only line most coaches click.\n\nUpdate it every season. A CV from last year reads like you stopped caring.\n\nSave it as a PDF named [Last name]-[First name]-[Birth year].pdf. Never a Word file, never a photo of a page.\n\nAsk your coach references before you list them. Every time.', 10),
    (cid, 5, 'Film', 'One tape a season', E'Three to four minutes, your best five clips in the first forty seconds. League games only: a director wants to see you against the rung you are on, not at a camp.\n\nWHAT GOES IN\n\nYour position''s job, over and over. A striker''s tape is goals and the runs before them. A center back''s tape is duels, headers, first passes out. A midfielder''s tape is receiving under pressure and what happens next.\n\nEvery clip starts a few seconds before your touch so the coach sees the picture you saw.\n\nMark yourself at the start of every clip: a circle or an arrow for the first second, then gone.\n\nWHAT STAYS OUT\n\nMusic. Most coaches watch on mute, and the ones who do not want to hear the game.\n\nSlow motion, effects, titles between clips. One title card at the start with your name, birth year, position, club, email. Nothing else.\n\nClips from training. Game footage only. A trick against a cone proves nothing.\n\nAnything longer than four minutes. A coach who wants more will ask for the full game.\n\nTHE FULL GAME\n\nAlways have one full match available as a link. Unedited. Put the link under the tape link in every email. The tape gets the door open; the full game gets you through it.\n\nHOSTING\n\nUnlisted on a video site, link in the CV and every email. Check the link works from a phone that is not yours before you send it.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nTitle it with the birth year, not the grad year: [Name] [Position] [Birth year] | Fall 2026.\n\nEvery season, newest clips first.\n\nThe full game matters. Directors watch what you do without the ball.\n\nFilm is the reason a director comes to a game. It is never the reason you get in. The game is.', 10),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'Email to a director', E'For an open tryout, an ID session, or to ask for a look. Sent by the player, with a parent copied for anything under 18.\n\nSUBJECT: [Birth year] [Position] | [Current club, league] | Tryout or ID session\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nMy name is [First Last], a [birth year] [position] with [club] in [league]. I am writing to ask about a tryout or ID session with [club or academy] for the [season] season.\n\nTape: [link]\nFull game: [link]\n\nMy next games: [date], [opponent], [venue], [time], number [number].\nMy current coach, [name], is happy to speak with you: [phone].\n\nThank you for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Birth year], [Position]\n[Parent name], [parent phone] (copied)\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nGive them a game to watch before you ask for a tryout. A scout who has seen you play arrives at the tryout already decided.\n\nUnder 150 words. Links do the talking.', 10),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'Before a game they can watch', E'A league game is the event here. Send this five to seven days before, and again the morning of, two lines only.\n\nSUBJECT: [League game or event], [date] | [Your name], [birth year] [position]\n\nTEMPLATE, A WEEK OUT\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nI wanted to let you know I will be playing [opponent] in [league] on [date].\n\n[Team] vs [opponent]\n[Day, date], kickoff [time]\n[Venue], field [number]\nJersey number [number], [position]\n\nMy tape is here if useful before the game: [link]\n\nI would love for you to watch. Thank you.\n\n[First Last]\n[Phone]\n\nTEMPLATE, THE MORNING OF\n\nCoach [Last name], playing today at [time] on field [number], number [number]. Hope to see you there. [First Last]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nGive them everything they need to find you in ten seconds: field, time, number. Coaches walk between six fields.\n\nNever ask if they are coming. Tell them where you will be.\n\nIf the schedule changes, send one more line. A coach who walks to the wrong field does not come back.', 20),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'After a game they watched', E'Send this within 24 hours. Even if you played badly. Especially if you played badly.\n\nSUBJECT: Thank you for watching, [event]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nThank you for coming to watch [team] vs [opponent] on [date].\n\n[One honest line about the game: what went well, or what you know you need to fix. Never an excuse.]\n\nI am very interested in [school or club]. If you have any feedback, or if there is a next step you would like me to take, I would be grateful to hear it.\n\n[Updated tape link, if you have new clips from that game]\n\nThank you again for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Phone]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNever blame the referee, the pitch, a teammate or the coach. A coach who reads an excuse stops reading.\n\nOne honest line about your own game shows more maturity than any highlight.\n\nIf they gave you feedback at the field, repeat it back: "You mentioned my first touch under pressure. I am working on it." Coaches remember who listened.', 30),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'The follow-up when nobody replies', E'Most coaches do not reply to the first email. That is normal. Send this two weeks after the last one.\n\nSUBJECT: Following up | [Your name], [birth year] [position]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nI wanted to follow up on my email from [date]. I remain very interested in [school or club].\n\nSince then: [one new thing, true: a new tape, a result, a league game they can watch].\n\nTape: [link]\n\nThank you for your time.\n\n[First Last]\n[Phone]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAlways bring something new. "Just checking in" with nothing new gets deleted.\n\nTwo follow-ups, two weeks apart. After that, leave it until you have a real update or a new event near them.\n\nNo reply is not a no. Coaches are not allowed to reply to younger players at some levels, and they still read. Keep the emails coming at each real update.', 40),
    (cid, 6, 'Outreach', 'After a tryout or ID session', E'Send this the same evening or the next morning.\n\nSUBJECT: Thank you, [tryout or ID session], [date]\n\nTEMPLATE\n\nCoach [Last name],\n\nThank you for [the tryout, the ID session, the time you gave me] on [date].\n\n[One specific thing you took from it: a drill, something they said, something about the program you saw.]\n\n[If true: After seeing it in person, [club or academy] is where I want to play.]\n\nMy tape: [link]\n\nI will keep you updated as the season goes. Thank you again.\n\n[First Last]\n[Birth year], [Position]\n[Phone]\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nName the specific thing. "Great session" says nothing. "The possession game in the second session showed me how you want center backs to play" says you were paying attention.\n\nSay you want it only if you mean it. Coaches compare notes.', 50),
    (cid, 7, 'The tryout', 'On the day', E'Everything a tryout decides, it decides early.\n\nBEFORE\nArrive early enough to warm up properly. Know your number and your group. One parent, quiet, on the far side.\n\nTHE FIRST TEN MINUTES\nSimple and sharp. First touch clean, pass to the right foot, talk. The coaches are sorting by then.\n\nTHROUGH THE SESSION\nPlay your position the way your club coach coaches it. Communicate in every drill. Lose a ball, win it back. No tricks nobody asked for.\n\nAFTER\nThank the coach who ran your group, by name. Email that evening: the After a tryout or ID session template under Outreach.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nThey are watching how you handle a bad touch more than the good ones.', 10),
    (cid, 8, 'Age and grades', 'Age matters earlier', E'The college route forgives a late start. The academy route mostly does not.\n\nWHAT THAT MEANS\n\nAcademies pick by birth year, and most places on the top rung are taken young. If this is the target, the work is now, in the league you are in, not next season.\n\nA late start is not a closed door. It means the rung-by-rung route, done fast, with the film to show for each rung.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAsk us where you stand for your birth year. We will tell you straight, and the answer changes the plan, not the goal.', 10),
    (cid, 8, 'Age and grades', 'Grades still matter', E'Academies read report cards. A player with school problems is a problem they do not need.\n\nWHAT THEY LOOK AT\n\nGrades, attendance, and whether the school will work with the training schedule. A pro academy place often means a changed school arrangement, and they will not make it for a player who is already behind.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nKeep the transcript ready the same way the college route does. The college door should stay open even while you chase this one; most academy players end up using both.\n\nTell us if grades are slipping. That gets fixed before any director hears your name.', 20),
    (cid, 9, 'The pro academy', 'How the MLS academies work', E'Most MLS academy players come from the clubs below them: the MLS NEXT teams, the affiliate and pathway clubs, and the academy''s own ID events. Being seen week after week in your league is the main route in.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nAsk the question early: which clubs feed the academy nearest us, and is mine one of them? Your club director knows.\n\nAn academy place changes school, travel and family time. Ask about all three before you ask about the shirt.\n\nCost differs by club. Ask plainly what the family pays and what the club covers. Get it in writing.\n\nNobody can promise an academy place. What the work earns is a clear path and a player ready for the moment.', 10),
    (cid, 9, 'The pro academy', 'When an offer comes, ask', E'Ask these out loud, the player asking, before anyone says yes.\n\nHow much will I play in my first season?\nWhat are the training days and the travel?\nHow does school work, and who arranges it?\nWhat does the family pay, and what does the club cover?\nWhat happens if it does not work out after a season?\nWho left this group last year, and why?\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nGet the answers in writing. A director who will not write it down has told you the answer.\n\nAsk us before you say yes. We have seen these offers and we will tell you what the words mean.', 20),
    (cid, 10, 'Overseas', 'Before you chase overseas', E'FIFA restricts international moves for players under 18, with narrow exceptions. A real overseas path almost always starts at 18 or through a family''s own circumstances abroad. Anyone promising a club overseas to a 15 year old is selling something.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNever pay anyone up front for a trial, an agent, or an introduction. Real routes run through coaches who know coaches.\n\nA trial abroad is a trial, not an offer. Go with a return ticket and a plan for school.\n\nAsk us before you say yes to anything overseas. We have walked this road and we will tell you straight.', 10),
    (cid, 11, 'The rules', 'The rules nobody tells you', E'THE PLAYER DOES THE WORK\n\nThe player writes every email, with a parent copied for anything under 18. The player talks to the coach after the tryout. A director who hears from a parent first assumes the player does not want it enough.\n\nParents: proofread, drive, pay for the tape, stay on the far side of the field. That is the job.\n\nTIMING\n\nEmail a director when you have something: a league game they can watch, a new tape, a result. Not on a schedule.\n\nA week before a game, then the morning of. Within a day after. Two weeks later if silence. Then only with news.\n\nBEFORE THE FIRST EMAIL\n\nMinutes in your league, a tape from those games, the full game, the report card, and a club coach who will take the director''s call. One weak link and it stops there.\n\nSOCIAL MEDIA\n\nAssume every director reads your accounts before they reply. Clean it up before the first email, not after.\n\nWHAT WE NEVER PROMISE\n\nNobody can promise an academy place, not us, not anyone. What the work promises is a clear path and a player ready for the moment. That is the whole job.', 10),
    (cid, 11, 'The rules', 'The tracker', E'One row per club. Update it the day anything happens. If it is not on the sheet, it did not happen.\n\nCOLUMNS\n\nClub | Rung (the one above you, two above) | Director or coach name | Email | First email sent | Replied (date) | Game they watched | Last contact | Next step | Next step date | Notes\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nNext step is never blank. Even "follow up Nov 12" or "send fall tape when done" counts.\n\nRead the sheet every Sunday. Five minutes. Do whatever is due that week.\n\nEvery club on the rung above you, and the two or three on the rung above that. When one goes cold, keep it on the sheet; directors move clubs.\n\nThe sheet belongs to the player. The parent can see it. The player keeps it.', 20),
    (cid, 12, 'The parent''s job', 'The far side of the field', E'Drive, pay, proofread, stay on the far side of the field.\n\nWHAT HELPS\n\nCopied on every email, writing none of them. Keeping the tracker honest. Asking the school and money questions when an offer comes, because those are yours to ask.\n\nWHAT HURTS\n\nCoaching from the sideline. Directors notice, and not in a good way. Emailing a director yourself. Talking to the coach after a tryout before your player has.\n\nRULES OF THUMB\n\nA director recruits a family, not a player. Be the family they want in the group.', 10);
  select count(*) into placed from public.elite_course_steps where course_id = cid;
  if placed <> 20 then
    raise exception 'Academy Recruiting seed placed % of 20 pages', placed;
  end if;
end $$;

-- The three courses these replace. Off, not gone; the coach deletes them
-- in the editor once the new two are read.
update public.elite_courses
   set active = false, sort = sort + 900
 where slug in ('recruiting', 'college', 'academy')
   and active;

